    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.2172/3017519",
    "abstract": "The past two decades have witnessed natural disasters and extreme weather events that affect millions of people. At the same time, the data volume from high-resolution climate models, satellite, in-situ and ground-based measurements have substantially increased to petabyte scales. These new and readily accessible datasets create the previously missing pipeline required for scientific machine learning (ML) and therefore new opportunities for improved understanding and prediction capability of climate extreme events. This project developed a deep latent variable model framework to discover physically meaningful hidden structures from high-dimensional, spatiotemporal climate extreme data.",
    "field": "Machine learning for climate science",
    "problem": "This research addresses challenges in modeling climate extremes, including sparse/irregular data and model misspecification, by developing novel deep latent variable models to discover physically meaningful spatiotemporal and causal structures from complex climate data.",
    "method": "The project developed a framework of deep latent variable models, including spatiotemporal point processes and multi-fidelity neural processes, to forecast irregularly sampled events and discover meaningful structures from complex climate extreme data.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and apply deep latent variable models and other machine learning methods to discover physically meaningful structures from climate extreme data and improve forecasting of irregularly sampled events.",
